    "result_text": "The change is implemented and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added per-country national-number length validation to the E.164 telephone normalizer in `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/OwnerTelephone.java`.\n\n**What changed:** After a raw telephone is cleaned and assembled into its E.164 digit string (and after the existing generic 8\u201315 digit check), `toE164` now matches the leading country code against a table of required national-number lengths:\n\n- `+61` (Australia) \u2192 9 national digits\n- `+1` (NANP) \u2192 10 national digits\n\nThe table is a `LinkedHashMap` ordered longest-code-first so the most specific prefix wins. When the national number (E.164 digits minus the country-code digits) doesn't match the required length for a recognised country code, it throws the existing checked `InvalidOwnerTelephoneException`, which the endpoint's escalation handler already turns into a **400**. Country codes not in the table keep falling back to the generic length check, so unrelated numbers (and legacy seed data read via `canonical`) are unaffected.\n\nThe new acceptance test (`+61 123` \u2192 400, since `+61` requires 9 national digits) passes, and `ownerNode()`'s valid `04XXXXXXXX` telephone still normalizes to `+61` + 9 national digits as before. `BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests green.",
